WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.122
Missing text and blank text are not the same thing in PostgreSQL.

00:00:04.122 --> 00:00:06.837
NULL means no known value.

00:00:06.837 --> 00:00:11.417
An empty string is a real text value containing zero characters.

00:00:11.417 --> 00:00:18.027
A string made only of spaces is also a real value until those spaces are removed.

00:00:18.027 --> 00:00:24.237
This matters when the business rule says something like “the patient has no usable email address.”

00:00:24.237 --> 00:00:29.082
If we test only email IS NULL, we will miss an empty string.

00:00:29.082 --> 00:00:32.912
We will also miss a value that contains three spaces.

00:00:32.912 --> 00:00:40.102
From the user's perspective, all three may mean “no usable email,” but the database sees them differently.

00:00:40.102 --> 00:00:43.937
A useful normalization pattern is to trim the text first.

00:00:43.937 --> 00:00:47.477
BTRIM removes leading and trailing whitespace.

00:00:47.477 --> 00:00:51.462
Then NULLIF compares that cleaned value with an empty string.

00:00:51.462 --> 00:00:54.837
If they are equal, NULLIF returns NULL.

00:00:54.837 --> 00:01:00.772
After that, IS NULL can treat missing, empty, and whitespace-only values consistently.

00:01:00.772 --> 00:01:04.617
This pattern appears outside healthcare all the time.

00:01:04.617 --> 00:01:07.607
Customer phone fields, product descriptions, survey comments, usernames,

00:01:07.607 --> 00:01:14.907
and address lines can all contain blank-looking values that are technically not NULL.

00:01:14.907 --> 00:01:19.812
The deeper lesson is to separate storage representation from business meaning.

00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:27.037
The database may store three different technical forms, while your analytical rule may want to treat them as

00:01:27.037 --> 00:01:28.114
one concept: “not usable.”
